<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Ideas That Shaped Modern Politics</h1><p class="path-tagline">Liberalism, conservatism, nationalism, socialism and fascism, read beside the thinkers who framed them.</p><p class="path-intro">Most of the political ideologies still argued over today took shape between the late 17th and the early 20th centuries. Liberalism grounded lawful government in the consent of the governed, and conservatism answered the French Revolution with tradition, authority and hierarchy. Nationalism held that each nation should govern itself, socialism and communism called for common ownership, and reformists sought equality through gradual democratic change. Fascism, born in Italy during World War I, exalted the nation above all and drove the Axis powers to war and genocide.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Timekeeper</a></p><p class="path-print-row"><button type="button" class="ws-print path-print">Print this path</button></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/john-locke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-locke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1632–1704</span><span class="path-step-name">John Locke</span><span class="path-step-why">Locke argued that government rests on the consent of the governed and must protect life, liberty and property, and his Two Treatises shaped liberal political thought.</span><span class="path-step-lede">John Locke was an English philosopher whose Two Treatises of Government and Essay Concerning Human Understanding, published around 1689, shaped liberal political thought and empiricism.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/ideology/liberalism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-locke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · c. 1690 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Liberalism</span><span class="path-step-why">Arising in 17th-century England and the Enlightenment, liberalism was used to justify the Glorious, American and French revolutions, and later split into classical and social forms.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Liberalism is a political and moral philosophy of individual rights, liberty, consent of the governed and equality before the law, first systematised by John Locke and carried by the revolutions of 1688, 1776 and 1789.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-burke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1729–1797</span><span class="path-step-name">Edmund Burke</span><span class="path-step-why">A Whig who backed conciliation with the American colonies, Burke became a founding figure of modern conservatism with his Reflections on the Revolution in France of 1790.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Edmund Burke was an Irish-born statesman, orator and political thinker, educated at Trinity College Dublin.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/ideology/conservatism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-burke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Profile · c. 1790 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Conservatism</span><span class="path-step-why">Conservatism defends the established institutions of its time, in forms ranging from Disraeli's One Nation Toryism and Bismarck's welfare state to Salazar, Franco and Christian democracy.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Conservatism is a philosophy and ideology that seeks to preserve traditional institutions, customs and values, founded as a modern tradition by Edmund Burke in his opposition to the French Revolution.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/ideology/nationalism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · c. 1772 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Nationalism</span><span class="path-step-why">Nationalism holds that a nation should govern itself; it drove Italian unification and the independence of Africa and Asia, and is often cited as a cause of both world wars.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Nationalism is the political idea that a nation, defined by shared language, culture or history, should possess its own state or self-government.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Work · 1848</span><span class="path-step-name">The Communist Manifesto</span><span class="path-step-why">Published in London in February 1848, the pamphlet of Marx and Engels cast all history as the history of class struggles and predicted a classless society.</span><span class="path-step-lede">The Communist Manifesto, originally the Manifesto of the Communist Party, is a political pamphlet by Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels, commissioned by the Communist League and published anonymously in German in London in February 1848.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/ideology/communism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-communist-manifesto.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1793 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Communism</span><span class="path-step-why">From the October Revolution of 1917 communist governments spread until more than a third of the world lived under them, marked by one-party rule and the suppression of opposition.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Communism is the ideology of a classless, stateless society based on common ownership.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eduard-bernstein.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1850–1932</span><span class="path-step-name">Eduard Bernstein</span><span class="path-step-why">Bernstein's Evolutionary Socialism (1899) disputed Marx's predicted collapse of capitalism and argued for gradual reform, which the SPD condemned but largely followed in practice.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Eduard Bernstein was a German social democratic politician and socialist theorist, known for the reformist challenge to Marxism called evolutionary socialism or revisionism.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1883–1945</span><span class="path-step-name">Benito Mussolini</span><span class="path-step-why">Mussolini founded Fascism and ruled Italy as dictator from 1922 until 1943, invading Ethiopia in 1935 and allying himself with Nazi Germany.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Benito Mussolini was an Italian politician who founded Fascism and ruled Italy as dictator from 1922 until 1943.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/ideology/fascism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-on-rome.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1915 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Fascism</span><span class="path-step-why">Fascism sought a totalitarian one-party state; the wars and genocides of the Axis powers, the Holocaust above all, left it almost universally condemned after 1945.</span><span class="path-step-lede">Fascism is an authoritarian nationalist ideology that arose in Italy after the First World War under Benito Mussolini.</span></span></a></li></ol>
